# FMCG Sales Data Pipeline — Databricks Medallion Architecture

An end-to-end data engineering pipeline built on **Databricks** that ingests sales data from two source systems (a parent company and an acquired child company), cleans and models it through a **Bronze -> Silver -> Gold (medallion) architecture**, merges the child company's data into the parent's unified analytics layer, and serves it to a BI dashboard.

## Architecture

S3 (raw CSVs, RAW + Archive) -> Lakeflow Jobs -> Bronze Layer -> Silver Layer -> Gold Layer (Child) -> merge into Gold Analytics Table (Parent org) -> Serving Layer (Dashboards / Genie).

Governed end-to-end through Databricks **Unity Catalog** (`fmcg` catalog, `bronze` / `silver` / `gold` schemas), with ingestion orchestrated through **Lakeflow Jobs**.

## What this project does

Two companies, two sets of raw sales data, one warehouse:

- **Parent company** -- a full historical dump of customers, products, gross pricing, and orders, loaded once and then updated incrementally.
- **Child company** (recently acquired) -- its own customers, products, and pricing files, plus daily order CSVs landing in S3 that need to be picked up and merged into the parent's gold layer over time.

The pipeline standardizes both sources into a shared schema, resolves data quality issues (duplicate keys, inconsistent casing, mixed date formats, malformed IDs), and produces a single **enriched, denormalized `fact_orders` view** that a dashboard queries directly.

**Bronze layer** -- raw CSVs are read from S3 exactly as they arrive (`inferSchema`, file metadata captured for lineage) and written as Delta tables with Change Data Feed enabled.

**Silver layer** -- per-entity cleaning:
- `dim_customers` -- drop duplicate customer IDs, fix city typos, title-case names
- `dim_products` -- drop duplicates, normalize inconsistent title casing (`energy bars` -> `Energy Bars`)
- `dim_gross_price` -- parse `month` from multiple inconsistent date formats into one standard format
- `fact_orders` -- drop rows with missing order quantity, sanitize non-numeric customer IDs

**Gold layer** -- the child company's cleaned fact table is merged with the parent's gold analytics table (`fmcg.gold.fact_orders`). New arrivals land via `COPY INTO` for incremental loads; a `dim_date` table is generated programmatically (one row per month) to support time-based rollups.

**Serving layer** -- a denormalized SQL view (`vw_fact_orders_enriched`) joins `fact_orders` against the date, customer, product, and pricing dimensions and computes `total_amount_inr`, feeding a BI dashboard directly.

## Tech stack

| Layer | Tools |
|---|---|
| Compute / platform | Databricks (Lakeflow Jobs, Unity Catalog) |
| Processing | PySpark, Spark SQL |
| Storage format | Delta Lake (with Change Data Feed) |
| Source storage | AWS S3 |
| Ingestion pattern | Full load + incremental load (`COPY INTO`) |
| Dashboarding | SQL view + BI dashboard |

